---
/**
 * /adult-drivers-ed/texas/what-the-course-does-not-do — the trust page for the ADE cluster.
 *
 * Rebuilt 2026-10-13 on the hook → answer → loop → funnel recipe (Jackson, ~/Claude/SEO/ADE-PAGE-RECIPE.md).
 * This page is the cluster's damaging admission: it answers "does adult drivers ed give you a license" and
 * "does it replace the driving test" with a plain no, then states the one thing the course does, in the only
 * approved wording (CLAIM, §521.167). §84.40(j): never promise a license. Keep it plain; do not sell on it beyond
 * the band.
 */
import AdeGuide from '../../../components/ade/AdeGuide.astro';
import { itd, CLAIM, law } from '../../../data/ade-eligibility';
import { dpsStats as S } from '../../../lib/dps-stats';

const base = '/adult-drivers-ed/texas';
const url = `${base}/what-the-course-does-not-do`;

// The one-breath answer: lede and meta description say the same thing.
const answer = `Texas adult drivers ed ends in a certificate, not a license, and the road test stays. Its final exam ${CLAIM}.`;

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Does adult drivers ed give me a Texas driver license?', a: 'No, and no provider may promise that. The course produces a certificate. DPS issues licenses, after its own tests and its own checks.' },
  { q: 'Does the course teach me to drive?', a: 'No. It is classroom instruction: law, signs, right-of-way, risk. Learning to operate a car happens behind the wheel, with a driving school or supervised practice. Any online course claiming otherwise is misdescribing itself.' },
  { q: 'Does adult drivers ed replace the Texas driving test?', a: `No. Its final exam ${CLAIM}, and the driving skills test is untouched. That test is the step with a median ${S.driveTest.median}-day queue.` },
  { q: 'Does it replace the ITAD video?', a: `No. DPS says directly that the ITD programs do not replace the distracted driving module in the six-hour course. They are separate requirements and you do both.` },
  { q: 'Will it get me an insurance discount?', a: 'It depends on your insurer, and it varies by carrier and by age. Adult driver education is a licensing requirement rather than a discount product, so ask your insurer before you count on one.' },
  { q: 'What does adult drivers ed actually do?', a: `If you are ${law.requiredBand}, it meets the driver education requirement, and DPS cannot issue your first license without it. At any age, passing its final exam ${CLAIM}, which means one fewer test sat cold. DPS calls the course "highly recommended" for first-time applicants over 25.` },
];

const next = [
  { href: `${base}/texas-driving-test`, title: 'The driving test', blurb: 'What the road test checks, and why the course does not replace it.' },
  { href: `${base}/impact-texas-adult-drivers`, title: 'The ITAD video and its 90-day clock', blurb: `A separate ${itd.adultLengthHours}-hour DPS requirement. Watch it second, not first.` },
  { href: `${base}/texas-driver-license-documents`, title: 'What to bring to DPS', blurb: 'Identity, lawful presence, Social Security number and two proofs of Texas residency.' },
  { href: `${base}/do-i-need-adult-drivers-ed`, title: 'Do you need the course at all?', blurb: 'It depends on your age and who issued your current license.' },
];
---
<AdeGuide
  title="Does Texas Adult Drivers Ed Give You a License? No. What It Does Not Do | Road Ready Safety"
  description={answer}
  url={url}
  eyebrow="The course"
  h1="Texas adult drivers ed does not give you a license or replace the road test"
  lede={`${answer} It also does not teach you to drive, replace the ITAD video or move your DPS appointment. The full list is below, along with who does not need the course at all.`}
  faqs={faqs}
  dateModified="2026-10-13"
  updated="October 13, 2026"
  band={{ eyebrow: 'What it does do', heading: 'It completes the driver education requirement and the sign and law portions of the knowledge exam' }}
  next={next}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Tex. Transp. Code §521.161 and §521.167', href: 'https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/TN/htm/TN.521.htm' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Impact Texas Drivers (ITD) Program', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/impact-texas-drivers-itd-program' },
    { label: '16 TAC §84.40(j), which bars a provider from promising license issuance' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What does Texas adult drivers ed not do?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      It does not give you a license, teach you to drive, replace the road test or the ITAD video, or shorten any DPS queue.
    </p>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-4 pl-5 m-0 list-disc marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>It is not a driver license.</strong> It produces a certificate. DPS issues licenses, and Texas rules forbid a provider from promising you one.</li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>It does not teach you to drive.</strong> Six hours of reading cannot. If you have never been behind a wheel, budget for lessons separately; the lessons are the real work, and this is the paperwork around it.</li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>It does not replace the driving test.</strong> It {CLAIM}, and nothing more. The road test remains, with a median {S.driveTest.median}-day wait and {S.driveTest.over30} offices more than a month out.{' '}<a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/texas-driving-test">What the driving test checks</a>.</li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>It does not replace the {itd.adultName} video.</strong> DPS states this itself. They are two separate requirements that overlap in subject matter, and you do both.</li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>It does not shorten any queue.</strong> Finishing in one evening does not move your appointment. If the queue is your problem, <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/third-party-skills-testing">third-party testing</a> is the lever, not the course.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What does the course actually do?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Passing its final exam {CLAIM}. Under §521.167, passing an approved course means you are not required to
      take the highway sign and traffic law parts of the DPS examination, once a licensed driver education
      instructor confirms you passed them.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      If you are {law.requiredBand}, the course is not optional. DPS cannot issue your first license without the certificate at all.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Who does not need the course at all?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Anyone 25 or older, and new residents surrendering a valid, unexpired license from another state. At 25 and
      over it is a choice, which DPS calls &ldquo;highly recommended&rdquo; for first-time applicants, and we have
      written the case both ways on{' '}
      <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/drivers-ed-over-25">the page for drivers 25 and older</a>.
      Sometimes the answer is that you do not need us.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      If you are moving here with a valid license,{' '}
      <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/new-resident-out-of-state-license">the new-resident page</a>{' '}
      covers what DPS asks of you instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Why would a course provider publish this list?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Because the complaints in this industry are almost never about course quality. They are about someone who
      bought a course believing it did something it does not, found out at a DPS counter, and lost an appointment
      that was weeks out. Being clear up front costs us a few sales and saves a lot of people a bad afternoon.
    </p>
  </section>
</AdeGuide>
